WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:06.520
Let’s move on to a list, a collection of items&nbsp;
in a defined order and, in many cases, the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:15.400
common data structure used in Python apps. For&nbsp;
our example, contained in this 125-list file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.520
I have started a list of state capitals, line 1,&nbsp;
according to the list of states in alphabetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:27.680
order, with Montgomery being the capital&nbsp;
of Alabama, Juneau for Alaska, and onward.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:35.480
Notice the syntax for this list. Commas separate&nbsp;
values within the list, which is contained inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:42.120
of square brackets. The primary purpose of a list&nbsp;
is to store a list of values in a single variable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:48.240
The time-saving factor of having a list is that,&nbsp;
when you have a list of values you need to adjust,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:53.840
you don’t need to declare a variable for every&nbsp;
value. As we will see in the upcoming topic on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:00.400
list operations, it takes very little code&nbsp;
to add and remove from a list, thus, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:06.800
avoiding having to create multiple variables to&nbsp;
store multiple items. I will add one more state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:13.080
capital to this list, Phoenix. Of course, if you&nbsp;
want distinct values stored in code, you will want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:19.120
to avoid using a list. For example, if you make a&nbsp;
specific first and last name for someone playing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:24.320
game, you will want to store that information in&nbsp;
separate variables, as I have done here on lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:33.040
2 and 3. I will add a print statement on line&nbsp;
4 to print the list of capitals, and the list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:44.400
prints just as written, with values in quotes,&nbsp;
separated by commas, in a set of square brackets.

